                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                        "entry_id": "fafe26f6-d035-3e66-9cd3-eed5b0655937",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The amount an arm or arms can hold."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1bef5fbc-3fdd-407b-9ab6-2f546d143fa7",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "[T]ake three or four good arms full of muckle Straw, Hay, or Fern, not too wet, not to dry, and obſerving which ſide of the Orchard the Wind blows on, then laying a good armful of muckle in three or four places according to the bigneſs of your Orchard, then get ſome dry ſticks, and having kindled them put an armful of muckle upon the Fire, and it will ſmoak and ſmoother, and the wind will drive the ſmoak through the whole Orchard, continue it till the wind turn out of the Eaſterly quarter, and it will preſerve the Trees and Fruit from blites and all manner of flys and caterpillers, which thoſe ſorts of bliting winds uſually bring; [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "82292838-c35c-46b7-948f-9cecd0c9e4ad",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "Upon this, by Conſent, the Doctors put a Good Armful of Warm Womans Fleſh into the Bed to him, [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "573a39b1-aff2-494d-810a-2b96f548d5be",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "[T]hough Pyrocles and Muſidorus at other times would diſpence with the length of the ſports, yet now, in reſpect of the armfuls of joys they were to expect in bed, they thought them tedious; [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "94609ad1-3982-40e6-9d4e-dde520d2fa85",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "The Lodge is to be made after this Manner; [...] You muſt lay a good Handful of Straw under the Cord upon the Turf, to prevent dirting the Cord, as well as ſpoiling the Turf, and you had need to have a good Armful in your Lodge, to keep you warm and dry, as Occaſion requires; [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "69d2f5b6-189b-4358-8f05-9af1d69afc62",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "[I]t was thus the passion of Mr. Panton allayed its overflowings: for very shortly after his marriage, he again fell passionately in love with another lady; a bona roba Queen, the full head taller than himself, and more than an armfull."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff5a20ee-1b05-43bd-8948-05b44ceebb74",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "We have here a handful—an armful would be nearer the truth—on poems on scriptural and sacred subjects."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3df918dc-8cc1-4a9d-b5eb-a054efb71d97",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "In this state of the weather, and before sunrise, in the grey of the morning, we had to wade off, nearly up to our hips in water, to load the skiff with the wood by armsfull."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "23afccb0-a383-4cfe-bb37-d58a847a1e4d",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "In the morning they [sticks of tobacco] should be gathered up and placed in armfuls in a box made of twigs or slats from whence they should be taken for assorting, which should be in done a sheltered place."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4c064261-65c7-4c7a-93cb-9353dbcc039c",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "\"Oh, yes, yes, Mary; give Aunt Becky a few sticks of wood, so she can get through with her washing.\" / \"Well, how many sticks, father?\" / \"Oh, give her an armful; give her an armful, my child.\" / \"An armful, father? what, my armful or her armful?\" / \"Why her armful, of course, child.\" / \"Well, how big an armful? or how many sticks, father?\" / \"Why, my child, give her as much as she can carry in her arms.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e06d242c-d0f4-4be7-969f-5f39a2987c3d",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "Oh, Nabby, Nabby! do tell me what they are doing up at your church. I’ve seen ’em all day carrying armfulls and armfulls—ever so much—spruce and pine up that way, and Jim Brace and Tom Peters told me they were going to have a ’lumination there, and when I asked what a ’lumination was they only laughed at me and called me a Presbyterian."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6ed8ebf8-9fa5-40cd-beea-0d26d1d3ee35",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "Together we hurried the books into the new white shelves which awaited them, the order in which they stood being of no matter so long as they were off the floor. Armful after armful was hastily stacked, the only pause being when (in the curious way in which these things happen) my own name suddenly caught the eye of the foreman. \"Did you write this one, sir?\" he asked. I admitted it."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5deaaa4-cd92-4ff2-a0cd-07daadb72998",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "I left the plane with two armfuls of groceries, a gun, searchlight, and other things. It wasn't until I was 500 feet down that I realized the problem of pulling the ripcord with my arms full."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2df65fae-3196-49c4-b059-1442db83023a",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "When Vernon came in from work, he always brought me wildflowers he had found, an armsful of tiger lilies, bluebells, and white daisies."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f9d7533e-b8b7-4dcd-8213-74231c0c7bb9",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "Naomi Levine made a movie wherein little kids swatted each other with armsfuls of huge white flowers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0e38dc8-fda4-4dbf-96d9-8ddb065e0e04",
                                "sense_id": "a87a1484-4f79-332c-978b-19b9ea3dd929",
                                "text": "At one point, a man who looks after lost property for London Transport asks himself what the ‘armfuls and armfuls’ of mislaid umbrellas say about Britain: ‘It says caution, it says preparation, it says pragmatism. Quiet, ordered lives in which nothing could be more distressing than to get rained on. The umbrella, the safety net of the nation’ [...]."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
